<ContentLayout structuredData={__sd} title="Keyboard and mouse fit guide | StackGeist" description="A practical method for choosing keyboard width, mouse shape, handedness, placement, and software features around your body and desk." active="guides">
  <article class="article-page">
    <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/guides">Guides</a> / Keyboard and mouse fit</p>
    <p class="page-kicker">Input fit</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">Fit the reach zone before comparing switches and sensors.</h1>
    <p class="page-lead">Keyboard width changes mouse position. Mouse shape changes wrist and grip. Treat them as one desk system instead of two isolated product categories.</p>
    <div class="article-summary"><strong>The short version</strong><p>Keep the keyboard directly in front, the pointer close to it, shoulders relaxed, elbows near the body, and wrists in a neutral position. OSHA's workstation guidance uses the same placement principles.[3][4]</p></div>
    <div class="article-body">
      <section><p class="page-kicker">Start with width</p><h2>The numpad has a cost even when the keyboard fits on the desk.</h2><p>A full-size board moves a right-hand mouse farther outward. That may be worth it for frequent numeric entry, but it should be an explicit trade. OSHA notes that a keyboard without a ten-key pad can leave more room for the pointer and reduce reaching.[4]</p><div class="article-cards"><div><strong>Full size</strong><p>Best when the numpad earns permanent width and the mouse can remain close.</p></div><div><strong>TKL / 75%</strong><p>Keeps navigation keys while reclaiming useful mouse space.</p></div><div><strong>Compact</strong><p>Maximizes space, but layers and missing dedicated keys can add workflow friction.</p></div></div></section>
      <section><p class="page-kicker">Measure</p><h2>Mock the footprint before checkout.</h2><ol class="article-steps"><li><strong>Mark keyboard width.</strong><span>Use painter's tape or paper at the product's stated dimensions.</span></li><li><strong>Center the typing area.</strong><span>Center the letter keys to your body, not the entire full-size chassis.</span></li><li><strong>Place the pointer.</strong><span>Keep it close enough that the upper arm can remain relaxed.</span></li><li><strong>Run a real task.</strong><span>Type, scroll, select, and use shortcuts for ten minutes without staging a photo posture.</span></li></ol></section>
      <section><p class="page-kicker">Mouse fit</p><h2>Handedness, size, shape, and control matter more than maximum DPI.</h2><p>OSHA recommends selecting a pointing device for the hand that operates it, testing fit before long-term use, and adjusting sensitivity so the pointer can cross the screen while the wrist remains neutral.[4]</p><ul class="article-list"><li>Reject a right-handed sculpted mouse for left-hand use, even if every feature looks useful.</li><li>Compare dimensions and mass with a mouse you already understand.</li><li>Decide whether your normal grip is palm, claw, or fingertip instead of copying a reviewer's preference.</li><li>Check whether custom buttons require software that your work computer blocks.</li><li>For gaming, separate comfort and control requirements from office shortcut requirements.</li></ul></section>
      <section><p class="page-kicker">Keyboard height</p><h2>A premium board can still force a poor wrist angle.</h2><p>OSHA advises adjusting keyboard, chair, and work-surface height to support relaxed shoulders and neutral wrists, and not using keyboard feet when they increase wrist bending.[3] A tall mechanical case may fit better with a palm rest, a lower desk surface, or a different typing posture—but accessories cannot rescue a fundamentally wrong height.</p><div class="note">This is practical setup guidance, not medical advice. Persistent pain, numbness, weakness, or tingling deserves evaluation by a qualified health professional.</div></section>
      <section><p class="page-kicker">Shortlist</p><h2>Use product features only after the physical fit passes.</h2><div class="cards"><a class="card" href="/gear/input/keychron-v6-max"><h3>Keychron V6 Max</h3><p>Full-size, hot-swappable, and highly configurable—with a wide footprint to justify.</p></a><a class="card" href="/gear/input/logitech-mx-master-3s"><h3>Logitech MX Master 3S</h3><p>A right-handed productivity mouse whose shape and mass need a real fit check.</p></a></div></section>
      <section><p class="page-kicker">Sources</p><ol class="article-list"><li>[3] <a href="https://www.osha.gov/etools/computer-workstations/components/keyboards">OSHA — Computer Workstations: Keyboards</a></li><li>[4] <a href="https://www.osha.gov/etools/computer-workstations/components/pointer-mouse">OSHA — Computer Workstations: Pointer/Mouse</a></li></ol></section>
    </div>
  </article>
